 "brief": {
  "tone": "Austere, tense and intimate: a book of prophecy, kingship and fraught loyalty, with grief and dread in quiet interiors and bursts of violence on open fields.",
  "depiction_rules": [
   "Yahweh is never shown in human or animal form; depict only effects (a voice, light, thunder, the ark) rather than a visible deity.",
   "The ark of the covenant may be depicted as a gold-covered chest with cherubim, not as a person.",
   "Dagon, the Ashtaroth and the teraphim are idols and may be shown as statues without divine presence.",
   "The Philistine champion Goliath is a fully armed giant; the raw violence of the sling stone and beheading may be shown but without gratuitous gore.",
   "The Woman of Endor's necromancy is shown with Samuel as an old robed figure rising from the ground, not as a dramatic demonic spectacle.",
   "Nudity (Saul prophesying naked, 19:24) may be depicted with restraint."
  ],
  "era": {
   "name": "Late Bronze to early Iron Age Israel and Philistia, c. 11th century BCE",
   "people": "Israelite tribes (Ephraimites, Benjamites, Judahites) living as farmers and herders in hill country; Philistines as an organized city-state people with bronze and iron weapons; neighbors include Ammonites, Moabites, Amalekites and Edomites.",
   "dress": "Linen ephods for priests and boys; robes and mantles for elders and kings; bronze helmets and coats of mail for warriors; shepherds' bags and slings; simple woven tunics for common people.",
   "buildings_and_objects": "Mud-brick and stone houses with flat roofs; the tabernacle at Shiloh with doorposts and gates; altars of stone; the wooden ark overlaid with gold; threshing floors; watchtowers and strongholds; caves used as refuges.",
   "technology": "Bronze and emerging iron weapons and tools (iron spearheads, blacksmiths are rare among Israel); chariots and horses used by Philistines; oil lamps and horns for anointing; no writing of a full literary kind beyond scribal notes, but a book of royal law is written."
  }
